  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold low-level thicknesses and strong high pressure \nover the region will support frost/freeze impacts across all of\nthe mainland ENC this morning. Continued good radiational\ncooling through the beginning of the week will bring a freeze\nthreat each night/morning, although a season ending freeze this\nmorning would end the frost/freeze program for the counties the\nfreeze occurs, and additional frost/freeze headlines may not be\nneeded anymore. The only areas to escape frost or freezing temps\ntonight will be areas immediately adjacent to water bodies,\nincluding the beach communities. The risk of frost impacts may \nbe lessened Sunday night as a weak cold front moves through \nwhich will keep the boundary layer a bit more mixed with a light\nbreeze preventing ideal rad cooling. High pressure then \nreasserts itself Monday and Tuesday night, with freezing temps\nor frost returning once again.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The aforementioned very dry airmass and low TD's \neach day over the coming week will exacerbate the worsening drought \nconditions in place across ENC, and fortunately winds are not \nexpected to be strong with the high nearby. However, fire weather \nconcerns may grow with the curing vegetation combined with low RH \nvalues each afternoon. Highs will warm back to above climo in\nthe upper 60s to low 70s mid to late week, but overnight lows\nwill continue quite chilly and below climo with the dry high\npres cont to dominate all week.",
